{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Balaam saw that it was good, etc.</b> According to our Sages, may their memory be for a blessing (Midrash Rabbah), who explicitly explained the verse ״כִּי בָרוּךְ הוּא״ that HaShem said to Balaam — do not bless them — the intention is to say in this manner: ״טוֹב בְּעֵינֵי ה׳ לְבָרֵךְ״ — meaning, HaShem, who is mentioned in the preceding context and the name does not recur over Balaam, for HaShem had already said to him ״כִּי בָרוּךְ הוּא״ and he has no need to bless them. And although we find that he did bless them, this was to turn his curse into a blessing. And according to what I explained on the verse (Numbers 23:5) ״וַיָּשֶׂם ה׳ דָּבָר בְּפִי בִלְעָם״ — it was not Balaam who was blessing, but an angel was blessing them through his mouth, inserting the words into his mouth. And his saying <b>and he did not go as before, etc.</b> — meaning, he concluded that there is no hope from HaShem that He would agree to harm Israel; also, he did not go as before, etc., because there is no expectation from the serpents. Therefore he contrived an evil scheme, namely <b>and he set his face toward the wilderness</b> — meaning, to see what had provoked anger in the wilderness; perhaps through that path he would be able to curse." "<b>Or</b> it may be explained as the language of speech [dibbur], in the manner that the Zohar (part 3, 205a) explained the verse ״אָבִינוּ מֵת בַּמִּדְבָּר״ (Numbers 27:3) as the language of speech [dibbur], and the intention is because all the ten trials by which Israel tried HaShem were through speech: at the Sea of Reeds, with the calf, with the complainers, with the spies, in the congregation of Korah, the waters of Meribah, and so forth — all of them sinned through speech, and he reckoned that within the totality of Israel that stood before him there would be found many from the classes of those who spoke [medabberim] in the wilderness, and through that path he would curse." "<b>Further</b>, it intends according to their words, of blessed memory (Zohar Ḥadash 2:157a), who said that the wilderness is the place where the encampment of ס״מ the wicked one is stationed, and he intended to arouse his forces, and that is his saying ״וַיָּשֶׁת אֶל הַמִּדְבָּר פָּנָיו״ — for there is the head of the kelippah, ס״מ, and his legions, who hate the nation of the children of Israel. And it is precise in saying <b>his face</b> [panav] — perhaps so that the kelippah would be aroused through the medium of anger, just as holiness is aroused through joy and a good heart, for each one is aroused according to its kind, and that is what it says ״פָּנָיו״ in the language of anger, after the manner of its saying (Exodus 33:14) ״פָּנַי יֵלֵכוּ״, which is the language of anger." "<b>Or</b> it is possible to explain the verse in this manner: <b>and Balaam saw</b> that whoever is good is worthy in the eyes of HaShem to bless Israel, after the manner of its saying (Proverbs 22:9) ״טוֹב עַיִן הוּא יְבֹרָךְ״, and our Sages of blessed memory said (Sotah 38b): do not read יְבֹרָךְ but rather יְבָרֵךְ. And the intention in this is that because he was evil-eyed, as is well known, HaShem did not grant him to bless, as hinted in the statement ״כִּי בָרוּךְ הוּא״. And from this he sensed that he was disqualified for being evil-eyed, and the one who blesses is the angel who speaks through his mouth. Therefore he thought that he would not go time after time toward the serpents, which is the evil portion. <b>And he set toward the wilderness</b> — meaning, where Elohim camps there, the place of the encampment of the Shekhinah, and he rebelled against the root of his soul and thought that through this he would become fit to be good and to bless Israel. And the verse informs us that he did not do this of his own will and from his heart, but only superficially did he do this thing, intending to deceive the Searcher of Hearts. Or perhaps his thought was that the angel would trust him to remove the hook from his mouth to speak as he wished, and then he would devise schemes to cleverly arrange the order of the blessing itself for evil, as indeed he did, as they said of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 105a) in explanation of his saying ״כִּנְחָלִים וְגוֹ׳ כַּאֲהָלִים״ etc., and the holy spirit would cancel his thought and say: ״לֹא כְּמוֹ שֶׁאַתָּה חוֹשֵׁב״ etc."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:13:49.768831709Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}